From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 19:34:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] script: Resume a backed-off media fetch at the next byte
 instead of seeking

When the player has enough data, the media element cancels a seekable
fetch, and restarts it once the player asks for data again. It did not
know which byte the player expected next, so it restarted by seeking the
element to the current playback position. That queued seeking and seeked
events that the page never asked for, and made GStreamer flush its
pipeline and download again the data that it had already buffered. When
the media resource has no seekable range, for example while its duration
is unknown, the seek did nothing and playback stalled.

Track the offset of the next byte that the player has not accepted: the
start of the fetch plus the bytes that the player took, without a chunk
that it refused because it has enough data. Restart the fetch at that
offset, without a seek, and let the new fetch push its data right away,
since the player already asked for it. GStreamer's source only asks for
a new fetch when the pipeline seeks to an offset other than its own, and
its offset moves by the same bytes, so the restarted fetch simply
continues its stream. (A seek that the source declined reset its offset
to 0; a later patch fixes that.)

Cancel on the EnoughData event only once the next chunk arrives, rather
than at once, and ignore empty chunks, like the last frame of an HTTP/2
body, so that a fetch that already delivered all of its bytes is never
restarted at the end of the resource, where the server would answer
with an error.

The fetch context now keeps that position and whether the player has
enough data, which grows HTMLVideoElement from 1248 to 1256 bytes.

Fork note: meant for upstream. Drop once upstream has it.

Testing: no test; the player only refuses data once its 50 MiB queue is
full, which needs a much larger resource than a WPT test can use. See
Validation.

Validation: headless release builds for Linux (GStreamer 1.24) of ohos-main 02ebe6403d0 with and without this patch, playing a 40 s, 149.7 MB H.264 MP4 (30 Mbit/s, moov first) to its end in a muted `<video preload=metadata>`, which keeps GStreamer's download buffering off so that its 50 MiB source queue fills up. From a local server with range support: without this patch, 3 requests and 223.0 MB sent, restarted after seeking and seeked events at 13 s and 27 s; with it, 3 requests restarting at the byte offsets 72.6 MB and 125.6 MB, 165.5 MB sent (the excess is the bytes in flight when a fetch was cancelled), and no seeking event. On the first version of this patch, before review fixes that do not touch this path, a trace of GStreamer's source showed its offset reaching exactly each restart offset and never going back, appsrc seeking only once, to 0, at startup, and an `<audio>` element playing the same video-only file, whose duration is reported as infinite, play all 40 s in 3 requests, where it stalls at 19.3 s after one request without this patch. Without range support: 3 requests from byte 0 either way (373.0 MB without, 375.0 MB with this patch), but no seeking event with it. `./mach test-wpt --release --processes 6 --timeout-multiplier 2` of html/semantics/embedded-content/media-elements and the-video-element (432 tests) with this patch and with an ohos-main build: no new failure. Its unexpected results either also occur without this patch (media_fragment_seek fails in 3 of 3 isolated runs either way, autoplay-default-permissions-policy, the mp4 subtest of video_timeupdate_on_seek passing in 1 of 5 isolated runs without it) or did not repeat in 3 isolated runs (gc-unreferenced-media-element). `./mach check --ohos` passes.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; written and tested by the agent, reviewed by another agent)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
---
 .../html/embedded_content/htmlmediaelement.rs | 106 +++++++++++++-----
 tests/unit/script/size_of.rs                  |   2 +-
 2 files changed, 79 insertions(+), 29 deletions(-)

diff --git a/components/script/dom/html/embedded_content/htmlmediaelement.rs b/components/script/dom/html/embedded_content/htmlmediaelement.rs
index fd06d951ff6..6ac1f03b2fd 100644
--- a/components/script/dom/html/embedded_content/htmlmediaelement.rs
+++ b/components/script/dom/html/embedded_content/htmlmediaelement.rs
@@ -1624,6 +1624,7 @@ impl HTMLMediaElement {
 
         *current_fetch_context = Some(HTMLMediaElementFetchContext::new(
             request.id,
+            offset.unwrap_or(0),
             global.core_resource_thread(),
         ));
         let listener =
@@ -2945,27 +2946,38 @@ impl HTMLMediaElement {
         }
     }
 
-    fn playback_need_data(&self) {
-        // The media engine signals that the source needs more data. If we already have a valid
-        // fetch request, we do nothing. Otherwise, if we have no request and the previous request
-        // was cancelled because we got an EnoughData event, we restart fetching where we left.
-        if let Some(ref current_fetch_context) = *self.current_fetch_context.borrow() &&
-            let Some(reason) = current_fetch_context.cancel_reason()
-        {
-            // XXX(ferjm) Ideally we should just create a fetch request from
-            // where we left. But keeping track of the exact next byte that the
-            // media backend expects is not the easiest task, so I'm simply
-            // seeking to the current playback position for now which will create
-            // a new fetch request for the last rendered frame.
-            if *reason == CancelReason::Backoff {
-                self.seek(
-                    self.current_playback_position.get(),
-                    /* approximate_for_speed */ false,
-                );
+    fn playback_need_data(&self, cx: &JSContext) {
+        // The media engine signals that the source needs more data. If the current fetch request
+        // was cancelled because the media engine had enough data, we restart it where the media
+        // engine stopped accepting data. Otherwise, we push the data that is queued for it.
+        let restart_position = match *self.current_fetch_context.borrow() {
+            Some(ref current_fetch_context) => match current_fetch_context.cancel_reason() {
+                Some(CancelReason::Backoff) => {
+                    Some(current_fetch_context.data_source().borrow().position())
+                },
+                Some(_) => return,
+                None => None,
+            },
+            None => None,
+        };
+
+        if let Some(position) = restart_position {
+            // The restarted fetch continues at the next byte that the player expects, so the
+            // player needs neither a seek nor a NeedData event before it accepts the data.
+            self.fetch_request(cx, Some(position), None);
+            if let Some(ref current_fetch_context) = *self.current_fetch_context.borrow() {
+                current_fetch_context
+                    .data_source()
+                    .borrow()
+                    .set_locked(false);
             }
             return;
         }
 
+        if let Some(ref mut current_fetch_context) = *self.current_fetch_context.borrow_mut() {
+            current_fetch_context.set_player_has_enough_data(false);
+        }
+
         if let Some(ref mut current_fetch_context) = *self.current_fetch_context.borrow_mut() &&
             let Err(e) = {
                 let mut data_source = current_fetch_context.data_source().borrow_mut();
@@ -2985,13 +2997,11 @@ impl HTMLMediaElement {
 
     fn playback_enough_data(&self) {
         // The media engine signals that the source has enough data and asks us to stop pushing bytes
-        // to avoid excessive buffer queueing, so we cancel the ongoing fetch request if we are able
-        // to restart it from where we left. Otherwise, we continue the current fetch request,
-        // assuming that some frames will be dropped.
-        if let Some(ref mut current_fetch_context) = *self.current_fetch_context.borrow_mut() &&
-            current_fetch_context.is_seekable()
-        {
-            current_fetch_context.cancel(CancelReason::Backoff);
+        // to avoid excessive buffer queueing, so we cancel the ongoing fetch request with its next
+        // chunk if we are able to restart it from where we left. Otherwise, we continue the current
+        // fetch request, assuming that some frames will be dropped.
+        if let Some(ref mut current_fetch_context) = *self.current_fetch_context.borrow_mut() {
+            current_fetch_context.set_player_has_enough_data(true);
         }
     }
 
@@ -4001,16 +4011,23 @@ struct BufferedDataSource {
     locked: Cell<bool>,
     /// Temporary storage for incoming data.
     buffers: VecDeque<DataBuffer>,
+    /// The offset in the media resource of the next byte that the player has not accepted yet.
+    position: u64,
 }
 
 impl BufferedDataSource {
-    fn new() -> BufferedDataSource {
+    fn new(position: u64) -> BufferedDataSource {
         BufferedDataSource {
             locked: Cell::new(true),
             buffers: VecDeque::default(),
+            position,
         }
     }
 
+    fn position(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.position
+    }
+
     fn set_locked(&self, locked: bool) {
         self.locked.set(locked)
     }
@@ -4037,7 +4054,14 @@ impl BufferedDataSource {
         while let Some(buffer) = self.buffers.pop_front() {
             match buffer {
                 DataBuffer::Payload(payload) => {
-                    if let Err(error) = player.lock().unwrap().push_data(payload) {
+                    let length = payload.len() as u64;
+                    let result = player.lock().unwrap().push_data(payload);
+                    // A payload refused with EnoughData has to be fetched again, while the player
+                    // moves past one that it failed to push.
+                    if result != Err(PlayerError::EnoughData) {
+                        self.position += length;
+                    }
+                    if let Err(error) = result {
                         warn!("Could not push input data to player: {error:?}");
                         return Err(error);
                     }
@@ -4079,6 +4103,8 @@ pub(crate) struct HTMLMediaElementFetchContext {
     cancel_reason: Option<CancelReason>,
     /// Indicates whether the fetched stream is seekable.
     is_seekable: bool,
+    /// Whether the player signalled that it has enough data, and has not asked for more since.
+    player_has_enough_data: bool,
     /// Indicates whether the fetched stream is origin clean.
     origin_clean: bool,
     /// The buffered data source which to be processed by media backend.
@@ -4091,14 +4117,16 @@ pub(crate) struct HTMLMediaElementFetchContext {
 impl HTMLMediaElementFetchContext {
     fn new(
         request_id: RequestId,
+        offset: u64,
         core_resource_thread: CoreResourceThread,
     ) -> HTMLMediaElementFetchContext {
         HTMLMediaElementFetchContext {
             request_id,
             cancel_reason: None,
             is_seekable: false,
+            player_has_enough_data: false,
             origin_clean: true,
-            data_source: RefCell::new(BufferedDataSource::new()),
+            data_source: RefCell::new(BufferedDataSource::new(offset)),
             fetch_controller: FetchController::new(request_id, false, core_resource_thread),
         }
     }
@@ -4115,6 +4143,14 @@ impl HTMLMediaElementFetchContext {
         self.is_seekable = seekable;
     }
 
+    fn player_has_enough_data(&self) -> bool {
+        self.player_has_enough_data
+    }
+
+    fn set_player_has_enough_data(&mut self, enough_data: bool) {
+        self.player_has_enough_data = enough_data;
+    }
+
     fn origin_is_clean(&self) -> bool {
         self.origin_clean
     }
@@ -4255,6 +4291,12 @@ impl FetchResponseListener for HTMLMediaElementFetchListener {
     }
 
     fn process_response_chunk(&mut self, _: &mut JSContext, _: RequestId, chunk: Bytes) {
+        // An empty chunk, like the last frame of an HTTP/2 body, must not cancel a fetch that has
+        // delivered all of its bytes.
+        if chunk.is_empty() {
+            return;
+        }
+
         let element = self.element.root();
 
         self.fetched_content_length += chunk.len() as u64;
@@ -4265,6 +4307,14 @@ impl FetchResponseListener for HTMLMediaElementFetchListener {
                 return;
             }
 
+            // Cancelling with the next chunk rather than on the EnoughData event makes sure that a
+            // fetch that delivered all of its bytes is not restarted at the end of the resource.
+            if current_fetch_context.player_has_enough_data() && current_fetch_context.is_seekable()
+            {
+                current_fetch_context.cancel(CancelReason::Backoff);
+                return;
+            }
+
             // Discard chunk of the response body if fetch context doesn't support range requests.
             let payload =
                 if !current_fetch_context.is_seekable() && self.content_length_to_discard != 0 {
@@ -4516,7 +4566,7 @@ impl HTMLMediaElementEventHandler {
             PlayerEvent::MetadataUpdated(ref metadata) => {
                 element.playback_metadata_updated(cx, metadata)
             },
-            PlayerEvent::NeedData => element.playback_need_data(),
+            PlayerEvent::NeedData => element.playback_need_data(cx),
             PlayerEvent::PositionChanged(position) => {
                 element.playback_position_changed(cx, position)
             },
